# One row per filed elimination, saying what a wider elimination would do to the composed figure it
# comes off, and whether the filing itself allows that reading. The reading eliminations/paired.sqlc
# chose decides the first; the claim's own `boundOrigin` decides the second.
#
# Taken bound by bound, a wider elimination gives a narrower figure
#   Subtract bound by bound, and the low rises as the elimination's low rises while the high falls
#   as its high falls. Widen the elimination at both ends and the composed figure narrows. A
#   composer less sure how much was double counted therefore files a more precise figure: the one
#   place in this model where knowing less gives a tighter answer.
#
# Crossed, a wider elimination gives a wider figure
#   Pairing the sum's low with the elimination's high sends the width the other way, so widening
#   the elimination widens the composed figure, as every other term here does. The crossed pairing
#   is not chosen for that reason: eliminations/paired.sqlc takes it only where bound by bound would
#   leave the claim out of order. The direction follows from that choice; it is not its motive.
#
# What allows the bound-by-bound reading is a filed answer
#   Reading an elimination bound by bound assumes its bounds move with the bounds of the figure it
#   is taken out of. A filing can say exactly that at its claim's `boundOrigin`: a derivation naming
#   `sharedParts` means the figure is a copy of one stated in the part it removes, so its bounds are
#   that part's bounds and the assumption is the filer's own. An absent origin says nothing sets
#   this bound, which neither supports the assumption nor contradicts it. So `licensed` is not a
#   verdict on a filing; it says which of the two the filer wrote down.
#
# A single-value elimination has no reading to make
#   Where the elimination is a single value the two pairings give the same result, so there is
#   nothing for this relation to tell apart, and `isotone` is NULL rather than either answer. That
#   is most of them, and reading the population without this column would report a split the
#   corpus does not make.
#
# Why this reports and never accuses
#   Neither reading is a defect, and neither state of `licensed` is. Bound by bound is right
#   wherever the elimination's bounds are the sum's own bounds, which is what a double count taken
#   out of a figure usually is, and the filings without that answer are correct under the reading
#   they state. A wider elimination narrowing the figure is worth counting because nothing else
#   here can see it, and worth counting rather than judging.
#
# The claim's own figures come along, and they show the key is right
#   `claim_seq` is a position, so a join on it returns rows whether or not it points at the right
#   claim: off by one, it still returns one claim per elimination, which is why a row count cannot
#   settle it. The figures settle it, so they are carried here, and
#   queries/observations/20-elimination-monotonicity.sqlc requires them to agree on every row with a
#   figure. A key checked only by how many rows it returns is not checked.
#
# The three origin columns are the evidence for the two worked out from them
#   `licensed` and `what_the_bound_rests_on` are both read off `origin`, `origin_absent` and
#   `origin_derivation`, so those three come along beside them. Nothing else reads them directly,
#   and they stay because this relation runs on its own: a verdict whose inputs are not in the row
#   can only be trusted, and `licensed` is there to be checked against the filing.
#
# A typed blank stays a row
#   An elimination filed with no figure keeps its row, with the width, the reading and `licensed`
#   NULL. The suspension grounds decide what it lifts; this relation only says what could be read.
-- eliminations/paired.sqlc against epistemics/claims.sqlc at the claim the eliminated quantity is.
SELECT e.composition, e.composed_layer, e.quantity,
       e.low, e.mode, e.high, e.unit, e.absent, e.reason,
       e.high - e.low AS width,
       e.crossed,
       e.claim_seq,
       c.low  AS claim_low,
       c.mode AS claim_mode,
       c.high AS claim_high,
       c.unit AS claim_unit,
       c.origin, c.origin_absent, c.origin_derivation,
       CASE WHEN e.low IS NULL OR e.high - e.low = 0 THEN NULL ELSE e.crossed END AS isotone,
       CASE WHEN e.low IS NULL THEN NULL
            ELSE c.origin_derivation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'sharedParts' END AS licensed,
       CASE WHEN e.low IS NULL      THEN 'no figure, so nothing to read'
            WHEN e.high - e.low = 0 THEN 'a point: both pairings are one arithmetic'
            WHEN e.crossed          THEN 'a wider elimination widens the composed figure'
            ELSE 'a wider elimination narrows the composed figure'
       END AS what_a_wider_elimination_does,
       CASE WHEN e.low IS NULL THEN 'no claim to carry an origin'
            WHEN c.origin_derivation IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'sharedParts'
                 THEN 'the filer says it is a copy of the part it removes'
            WHEN c.origin_absent IS NOT NULL THEN 'the filer says nothing sets this bound'
            WHEN c.origin IS NOT NULL        THEN 'the filer names who may change this bound'
            ELSE 'nobody said'
       END AS what_the_bound_rests_on
FROM      (
    :compose(eliminations/paired.sqlc)
) e
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(epistemics/claims.sqlc)
) c ON c.filing = e.composition AND c.seq = e.claim_seq
